read-storage-capacity-for-local-roots

Lane: cross

Unit: guard-session-storage-across-local-roots

ChildPatch: read-storage-capacity-for-local-roots

Form: GitDiff

Segment: schema

Purpose: Protect session progress and canonical history using the effective Codex home and harness filesystems while preserving capacity observations for the control center.

Requires: none

Provides: Share Linux usable-capacity measurements through the unauthenticated storage/capacity/read RPC and aligned bounded TUI refreshes, preserving app-scoped samples, stale-result rejection, unknown observations and real zero. Detect canonical JSONL replacement, removal, truncation, external append or rewrite at write and flush boundaries and make the first writer error terminal without reopening. Request a session stop when either configured root has less than 1 GiB available, capacity cannot be read, or persistence fails. Let already-started responses finish before native turn cancellation and session teardown, block follow-up model and tool execution, and report the storage cause visibly. Preserve the complete ordered canonical JSONL suffix after the first primary-writer failure, including records received through response completion and turn teardown. Save the same appendable payload in an independent Codex home sessions file or, if that write fails, a Codex harness sessions file. Deliver raw JSONL to the TUI for an automatic clipboard attempt regardless of disk outcome and a visible bullet warning with retained copyable recovery contents.

Boundary: Own shared root capacity, native-writer failure propagation and response-boundary storage protection. Preserve native locks, lazy creation, ephemeral sessions, normal resume, explicit user interruption, account refresh behavior and unrelated sessions. Do not migrate live data, replace the remote thread-store contract, add a permanent capacity cache or introduce configurable thresholds. Control Center owns layout and formatting; storage reads never run during rendering. Rescue files are fragments of the original session, not new sessions, and never enter normal session discovery or usage accounting. Do not repair or overwrite the failed original file, retry its append, change record identities or introduce a recovery-specific persisted format. Clipboard operations remain owned by the native TUI worker and report unconfirmed or failed delivery truthfully.

Verification: Protocol and configured-root RPC tests retain authentication independence and actual zero. Capacity checks cover each root and the strict 1 GiB boundary. Recorder regressions cover StorageFull, external changes, terminal failures and normal append semantics. Session and gated-response checks cover deferred failure during a response, immediate stopping without an active response, no follow-up request, explicit interruption, one terminal error, closed admission and native resource release. TUI refresh tests preserve stale-sample rejection, account recovery and disconnect clearing. Recovery tests concatenate an intact prefix and captured suffix through native session loading, preserving original timestamps, record order and paginated ordinals. Cover partial primary writes, failed materialization, post-failure queued records, Home success, Harness fallback, both destinations failing and unchanged original bytes. End-to-end tests retain the final response and abort records in the rescue payload; TUI tests verify visible bullet warnings, exact plain-text JSONL copy payloads and disk-independent clipboard attempts.

CrossGroup: control-center


